{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792694794,"nonce":"MrAiAn5vs+zX7gF0QF+VUq8UIHg=","message":"Controls and implementation:\n\nBoth original controls fail before implementation. The asymmetric squircle sample (35,23) was [76,0,179,255] instead of fully red, and faded sample (35,30) was [128,0,128,255] instead of [48,0,207,255]. Log: /private/tmp/blinc-rgba-properties-negative.log.\n\nA separate control removed only the shaped-group guard after fixing capture/shading. The direct scene passed, but its opacity group failed at (35,23): [26,0,229,255] instead of [128,0,128,255]. Log: /private/tmp/blinc-rgba-properties-group-negative.log. The temporary change is restored.\n\nRaw images now retain get_clip_data's per-corner exponents through a row pointer in image clip header info.z; zero preserves the existing uniform-exponent ABI image behavior. Fade rows use the closest positive fade within the active composite clip range. Distances retain native get_clip_fade's draw-time scale convention; rectangular/rounded fade owners supply their own push-time screen rectangle, independently of later scissors. Other fade-owner shapes keep the native effective rectangular-scissor fallback. Ancestor clips/fades remain stripped during scratch painting.\n\nThe renderer accepts five-row fade-only and six-row corner headers as well as the existing shape payloads. Tight origins translate fade bounds and the optional shape frame addressed by the header pointer. Layer composition uses a rectangular scissor for shaped raw-image corners, preserving the shape already baked rather than cropping it back to a circle. No shader struct, GPU image-instance or primitive layout change. Shared ClipPath/CSS enums are untouched.\n\nNew empty headers reserve their final size up front. Fade-only payload is five vec4 rows (80 bytes); per-corner payload is six rows (96 bytes), also sufficient when combined with a fade. Existing nonrectangular payloads reuse their fade header rows. Ordinary unfaded circular-rounded/rectangular or absent clips still allocate no rows. These are structural payload sizes, not allocator/timing benchmarks.\n\nFocused verification on native Metal: three new offscreen GPU cases and one CPU hash case pass in normal SSBO and forced data-texture modes. Coverage includes asymmetric squircle, bevel, scoop, square and notch corners; per-corner radii; opacity groups; static/motion/scratch and background/foreground ownership; DPI; all fade edges; inner scissors retaining original fade edges; fades combined with shaped rectangles and paths; actual tight bakes; ancestor-fade stripping; and cache hashes with only exponent/fade changes. Three new CPU integration cases pass for row contents/frame scaling, foreground merge/drain ownership, and fade scope. The existing allocation-free ordinary-header CPU control passes.\n\nOnly directly related regressions were run: the local-affine polygon image-frame case, ABI image clip/fade/layer cases and ABI image fit/mask/repeat mapping cases, in both image shader variants. Image shaders select only on has_storage_buffers, so no redundant VB variant was run. Primitive/glyph capacities were 1024 and build concurrency was two. Capability overrides are restored. Logs: /private/tmp/blinc-rgba-properties-{normal,dt}-*.log. After a final header-capacity-only reservation change, the focused metadata tests were rerun; shader and emitted row values did not change.\n\nNo workspace-wide, DSL, documentation or platform gates. Native clip-stack selection/intersection approximations and non-image path support are unchanged; browser/mobile pixels and performance benchmarks remain unverified.","files":null}]}